{
 "id": "2026-qian-city",
 "name": "City Birds - Seeing, Naming and Valuing Urban Birds",
 "description": "Faithful replication with the paper's own stimuli: a residents' survey on urban birds. Participants see photographs of nine common birds (Chinese Blackbird, Eurasian Tree Sparrow, Little Egret, Light-vented Bulbul, Spotted Dove, Long-tailed Shrike, Crested Myna, White Wagtail, Eurasian Hoopoe), tick the ones they have seen and try to name each. They then rate 14 statements on what birds give (beauty, song, inspiration, education, well-being, seed dispersal, nutrient cycling, pest control) and cost (noise, disease, dirt, ugliness, damage), a modified 14-item Nature Relatedness Scale, and answer five conservation questions, after demographics and four nature-activity frequencies. All item wording and answer options are taken from the paper's Tables 1-7, and the photos are the paper's Figure 1 (Wikimedia Commons, CC BY / CC BY-SA / public domain, credited in the page). Original study: 1,430 residents of Zhoushan, China, face-to-face, 2024; most agreed birds are pleasing to look at (79.6%), 58.7% named 4-6 species, and 75.6% said birds should be protected. Differences from the original study: (1) online and in English instead of a face-to-face paper questionnaire in Chinese, so names are typed and must be scored afterwards (the species list is in the question notes) instead of being checked on site by an interviewer; (2) an \"I don't know\" box was added for naming; (3) the photos are cropped from the paper's figure, so each keeps its panel letter; (4) the question stems for Q1-Q11 are short headings written from the paper's section descriptions, since the paper prints the items and options but not every stem.",
 "author": "Qian, Ma, Fu, Chen, Shen, Chen, Gui, Cai & Zhao",
 "year": "2026",
